{ "verdict": "changes", "issues": ["The belt-and-braces restore guard in FlaggedWorkStore.RestoreAsync is dead code. It probes whether .relay/config.json is tracked with `git ls-tree -q HEAD -- .relay/config.json` (FlaggedWorkStore.cs:192), but `-q` is not a valid `git ls-tree` flag — verified on git 2.54.0 it errors with exit 129 ('unknown switch `q''), and GitInvoker passes args verbatim. So `configTrackedAtHead` is always false and the guard `if (configTrackedAtHead && !File.Exists(configPath)) return RestoreResult.Unrestorable;` (lines 205-206) never fires. This defeats the guard's stated purpose (task item 4: 'surface a loud failure ... not silent ... instead of proceeding into a run with a deleted config'): a legacy lossy bundle restored via Resume — explicitly in scope since 'existing bundles on disk should still restore' — would still silently delete .relay/config.json and strip executable bits, the exact corruption this task fixes, instead of bailing to Unrestorable (which the call site in RelayDriver.FlaggedWork.cs:35-41 already handles by deleting the bundle and restarting from stage 1). No test covers the guard's Unrestorable path, which is why the bug slipped through. Fix: drop the invalid flag, i.e. `[\"ls-tree\", \"HEAD\", \"--\", \".relay/config.json\"]`."] }
